# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.so
*.egg-info/
.eggs/

# Virtual environment
venv/
.env

# OS
.DS_Store

# Claude Code session metadata
.claude/

# Project runtime outputs
logs/
*.log
adapters.bak.*/
adapters.testbak.*/
# Labelled snapshots: adapters.RESEARCHER_ITER20.bak and friends. The rule
# above only matches the timestamped form, and an adapter backup that slips
# past .gitignore is ~19 MB of per-user weights in the history.
adapters.*.bak/
adapters.*.bak.*/
# Catch-all, because the two rules above are a naming convention and a
# naming convention is not a guarantee. `adapters.8B-headmaster.pre-14b/`
# (a base-model swap on 2026-08-26) matched neither and would have been
# committed — personal LoRA weights, which is the exact class of leak the
# 2026-08-25 history rewrite existed to undo. Nothing under adapters* is
# tracked, so this costs nothing and does not depend on remembering a
# suffix.
adapters.*/

# Runtime scratch for execute_code (constants.SANDBOX_DIR). Holds whatever
# the agent was working on — scraped pages, extracted rows, quarantined
# input — so it is per-user data, not source.
sandbox/
benchmark_lora_*.json
training_data/*.bak.*
training_data/tmp*.yaml
adapters.premask.*
demo_skills/*
demo_finetune_loop.py
# Benchmark measurement results (bench/*.json and root benchmark_*.json) are
# tracked: they are the model-score records the memory notes and models.json
# descriptions cite, and a disk purge already ate nine of them. The datasets
# they were measured against (bench/data/) are large and re-downloadable, so
# those stay ignored.
bench/data/
Adapter_skills/*
adapters.9b-scrape/*
# Per-user data: every install generates its own config, notes,
# training corpus, and LoRA adapter on first run — never ship them.
# prompt.md is seeded from DEFAULT_SYSTEM_PROMPT in main.py on first run.
config.json
config.json.*.bak
config.json.bak.*
prompt.md
prompt.md.default
prompt.md.backup.*
prompt.md.bak.*
# The security policy and its shipped snapshot are both written on first run
# by symbio.app.security (see ensure_security_file); security.md is then the
# user's own file to edit, so publishing either one would overwrite it.
security.md
security.md.default
security.md.bak.*
cron_jobs.json
gateway.pid
daemon.pid
# The Merkle root over THIS install's adapters, and the per-adapter seals
# under it. Every install has its own adapters, so shipping one person's root
# would only ever tell someone else their own weights are wrong.
adapters_root.json
# ...and its signature. Per-install like the root it covers.
adapters_root.json.sig
# The age recipients/identity for a locked adapter. The identity file
# holds no secret (the key does the decryption) but it names this
# install's hardware, and the recipients file is this install's alone.
adapter_recipients.txt
adapter_identity.txt
daemon.sock
agent_memory.md
user_profile.md
# The assistant's own read of what it is for, and of how this user works.
# Per-install by definition — one person's soul.md is a description of them.
soul.md
# The consolidated read of how this user works — one stance per question,
# inferred from their own sessions. Same reason as soul.md above: it is
# about a person, and it is theirs.
constitution.md
# Runtime state of the rigid AWS training environment, rewritten by every
# graded attempt. The environment itself (aws_sim/*.py) is code, and tracked.
aws_sim/state.json
# Everything a RUN produces: the corpus the model earned, its attempt log, the
# LoRA checkpoints and the scored curve. Per-run and per-user by definition —
# the environment and the harness are code and are tracked, what a particular
# model learned in it is not.
aws_sim/self_taught.jsonl
aws_sim/attempts.jsonl
aws_sim/curve.json
aws_sim/data/
aws_sim/adapters_aws/
# cold_start.py writes a throwaway adapter per trial here. Missed on the first
# pass and 98 MB of LoRA weights reached a commit because of it — the ignore
# list has to cover every path a RUN writes to, not the ones I remembered.
aws_sim/cold/
# Scratch fixtures for the multi-step drive tests, generated by a script.
/puzzle/
/puzzle_*
# Standing instructions: the user's own style preferences, kept across
# sessions and served to the model as trusted text. Per-user by definition —
# see symbio.app.memory.save_standing_instruction.
standing_instructions.md
sessions/
notes/
training_data/
adapters/
# The archive the code writes role adapters into (ADAPTER_ARCHIVE_DIR, and
# ADAPTER_ARCHIVE_DIR/workers/<role>), plus the loose historical adapters moved
# under it. Every adapters.* form above is ignored and this one was not, so an
# archived worker — real trained weights — would have been committable. That is
# the case the 2026-08-25 history rewrite existed to undo.
adapters_archive/
# Where the loose historical adapters live. Deliberately NOT inside
# adapters_archive/, whose top level the code reads as ROLE NAMES —
# list_archived_adapters counted the two subdirectories put there as two
# archived adapters, which is what "Archived adapters: 2" on an empty archive
# was telling us.
adapters_history/
screenshots/
sandbox/caine_tools.py
sandbox/symbio_tools.py
golden_cases.json
mcp_modules/

# Telemetry: local feedback/pings/state must never be committed.
telemetry/

# Throwaway one-off scripts (tool-use seeding, manual training runner).
seed_tools.py
train_runner.py

# Where those one-offs now live. Ignored as a whole directory, not by name:
# every pattern that used to cover them was root-anchored (`/_*.py`,
# `/diag_*.txt`) precisely so it could not reach into the package, so moving
# the files one level down would have quietly made them committable — which
# for a corpus-regenerating script is the per-user-data leak the 2026-08-25
# history rewrite existed to undo.
/scratch/

# Superseded copies of the per-user files above: config.json.bak.*,
# prompt.md.bak.*, security.md.bak.*, and the adapters.testbak.* directories
# an interrupted pytest run leaves behind. Each is per-user for the same
# reason its live original is, so the directory is ignored as a whole.
/backups/

# Scratch copies conftest.py points the runtime at during a pytest run. They
# are removed on a clean exit; ignoring them keeps an interrupted run from
# leaving the working tree dirty.
sessions.suite/
logs.suite/
notes.suite/
worker_models.suite.json

# Warmed system-prefix KV cache (hundreds of MB, machine- and model-specific).
cache/

# SQLite stores (memory.db, logs/sessions.db, logs/planner.db, notes/tags.db).
# Every install creates its own schema on first run, and the contents are the
# user's conversations and notes — never ship them. The -wal/-shm siblings are
# live journal files and are worse than useless in a checkout.
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# Skill evaluation reports embed the skill's own steps and model replies.
skill_eval_*.json

# Crash mirror written by preserve_training_state so an interrupted run
# can be repaired by the next one.
.pytest-training-backup/
eval_*.json

# Scratch scripts: kept locally, not published. The pytest suite (root
# test_*.py, conftest.py, tests/, tests_mcp/) IS tracked — see the suite-wide
# isolation in conftest.py, which redirects every real store to scratch dirs.
# Every pattern is root-anchored on purpose — an unanchored `_*.py` would
# also match symbio/__init__.py and quietly untrack the package.
/_*.py
/eval_routing.py
/eval_toolcall.py
/diag_*.txt

# The same scripts after the root cleanup moved them into bench/ and tests/.
# Commit 48b27a8 ("Keep the tests local; stop publishing them") untracked them
# on purpose, and every pattern above is root-anchored, so the move alone
# would have quietly published them again.
bench/_*.py
bench/eval_routing.py
bench/eval_toolcall.py
bench/trial_agent_models.py
tests/_*.py

# Locally quantised model weights — gigabytes, and GitHub caps files at 100 MB.
models/
# Local LoRA experiment snapshots: per-user weights, never publish.
adapters.thinking-pretrain/
tool_baseline.json

# Wrangler CLI local credential cache (account id, personal email)
.wrangler/

# Build artifacts from `python -m build`.
dist/
build/

# Per-tool definitions and user-defined slash commands. Both are SEEDED from
# code on first run (symbio/app/tool_docs.py, symbio/app/commands.py) and then
# edited in place, exactly like prompt.md and config.json above — so what is
# on disk is this install's, and committing it would publish one person's
# edits as everyone's defaults.
/tools/
/commands/

# The agent's persistent browser profile: session cookies for whatever
# the user logged into. Never publish.
/browser_profile/

# Chromium writes a profile directory here when a launch is given the literal
# path "null" -- what a shell redirect meant as /dev/null leaves behind. It is
# browser state (Safe Browsing lists, NativeMessagingHosts, a first-party-sets
# db), so it is both junk and the same kind of thing browser_profile/ is
# ignored for.
/null/

# Design-canvas output. Artboards and a 2.5 MB rendered canvas that nothing in
# the package imports, nothing in the desktop app serves, and no test reads --
# the desktop window's real assets are symbio_desktop/static/. Kept on disk
# because it is the source for that design; not shipped, because a repo that
# carries it makes every clone pay 2.5 MB to run nothing.
/design/

# Stray npm artifacts. `package.json` here is literally `{}` and the lock file
# is six lines — the residue of an `npx`/`npm install` run inside the repo, not
# a dependency of anything. Nothing in the harness is JavaScript except the
# desktop app's own static files, which are served from symbio_desktop/static.
/package.json
/package-lock.json
